.\" Manual page for udp-broadcast-relay-redux (Rust port)
.\" GPL-2.0-or-later
.TH UDP-BROADCAST-RELAY-REDUX 1 "2026-11-21" "udp-broadcast-relay-redux 1.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
udp-broadcast-relay-redux \- relay UDP broadcasts (and IPv6 multicast) to other networks, forging the sender address
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B udp-broadcast-relay-redux
.RB [ \-d ]
.RB [ \-f ]
.RB [ \-s\ \fIIP\fR ]
.RB [ \-t\ \fIIP\fR ]
.RB [ --id\ \fIid\fR ]
.RB [ --port\ \fIudp-port\fR ]
.RB [ --dev\ \fIdev1\fR ]...
.RB [ --multicast\ \fIip\fR ]...
.RB [ --lang\ \fIen|zh\fR ]
.RB [ --log\ \fITARGET\fR ]
.RB [ --ipv6 ]
.RB [ --list-interfaces ]
.RB [ -h | --help ]
.RB [ --version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B udp-broadcast-relay-redux
listens for broadcast packets on the specified UDP port and forwards them to
each other given interface. Packets are sent such that they appear to have
come from the original broadcaster, or from the spoofing IP if
.B \-s
is used. When running multiple instances for the same port on the same
network, each must have a different
.B id
(it is encoded as IP TTL 64+id, which also identifies relay echoes).
.P
This is the Rust port of the C original with identical default behavior,
plus internationalization, graceful signals, interface hot-plug following
(Linux) and an opt-in IPv6 multicast relay.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-d
Enable debugging output (goes to stdout).
.TP
.B \-f
Fork to background. The parent exits immediately; in non-debug mode the
daemon closes its standard streams.
.TP
.BI \-s " IP"
Set the source IP of forwarded packets. The special values
.B 1.1.1.1
(use outgoing interface address + broadcast port) and
.B 1.1.1.2
(outgoing interface address + original source port) are honored as in the
original.
.TP
.BI \-t " IP"
Set the destination IP of forwarded packets.
.B 255.255.255.255
means the outgoing interface's broadcast address.
.TP
.BI --id " id"
Instance id, 1–99.
.TP
.BI --port " udp-port"
UDP port to listen on (1–65535).
.TP
.BI --dev " dev1"
Interface to manage. Repeat for several interfaces. Interfaces that do not
exist yet are picked up by the hot-plug monitor (Linux) as long as at least
one requested interface exists at startup.
.TP
.BI --multicast " ip"
Join this multicast group on every managed interface. Repeatable; IPv6
groups (containing ':') require
.BR --ipv6 .
.TP
.BI --lang " en|zh"
Language of user-facing messages. Defaults to the
.BR LC_ALL / LC_MESSAGES / LANG
locale. English output is byte-identical to the C original.
.TP
.BI --log " TARGET"
Error/log channel:
.B stderr
(default; byte-faithful),
.BR syslog ,
or
.BI file= PATH
(append; reopen on SIGHUP).
.TP
.B --ipv6
Also relay IPv6 multicast groups on the same port (dual-stack receive).
.TP
.B --list-interfaces
Enumerate candidate interfaces with their addresses and selection state,
then exit. Does not require root.
.TP
.B \-h\fR, \fB\-\-help
Show the full help and exit.
.TP
.B --version
Show version and exit.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGTERM\fR, \fBSIGINT
Graceful shutdown (exit 0).
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Reopen the log file (rotation) when
.B --log file=
is used.
.TP
.B SIGUSR1
Dump packet counters (received, forwarded, dropped, per-interface sent).
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
HDHomeRun discovery across two interfaces:
.B udp-broadcast-relay-redux --id 1 --port 65001 --dev eth0 --dev wlan0 --multicast 239.255.255.250
.TP
SSDP relay with Chinese messages and syslog:
.B udp-broadcast-relay-redux --lang zh --log syslog --id 2 --port 1900 --dev br-lan --dev eth1 --multicast 239.255.255.250
.SH REQUIRES
Root privileges (raw sockets). Linux; macOS supported via the BSD code path
(compile-checked), FreeBSD experimental.
.SH AUTHOR
UDP Broadcast Relay Redux Contributors; Joachim Breitner; Nathan
O'Sullivan. Rust port and i18n additions by the
udp-broadcast-relay-redux-rust contributors.
